  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 125 PM MDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows a weak shortwave trough near the \nUtah/Colorado border. This combined with developing cumulus clouds\nover the mountains and limited moisture, means isolated high- \nbased showers are still on track for this afternoon and early \nevening. On Saturday, the ridging that's been over the region will\nfinally transition southeastward and be centered over the \nSouthern Plains. Southwest flow aloft will prevail behind the \nridge and ahead of an upper level trough over the Pacific \nNorthwest. Warm temperatures will continue Saturday. Due to the \nincreasing flow aloft, breezy southwest winds are expected for \nSaturday. Like the past few days, isolated to scattered high-based\nshowers are expected over the mountains, with light precipitation\namounts at most.\u00a0\n\nThe upper level trough dives south across the western states \nSunday and Monday while remnants from Hurricane Rachel lift north \nacross the Desert Southwest and Great Basin. Widespread rain is \nexpected across the Great Basin and Desert Southwest, even \nreaching parts of western Colorado. However, models are in good \nagreement that any precipitation associated with the upper level \ntrough and remnants of Hurricane Rachel will stay well west of the\nforecast area. The mountains could see isolated to scattered \nshowers Sunday due to increased moisture and instability. \nTemperatures will stay well above normal Sunday with highs in the \nupper 70s to mid 80s. Come Monday, the temperature forecast \nbecomes highly uncertain over northeast Colorado. Surface low \npressure deepens over southeast Colorado and drags a backdoor cold\nfront into northeast Colorado. The timing of this front will \nimpact Monday's temperature forecast. The NAM indicates an early \narrival for the cold front over the northeast plains with \ntemperatures struggling to reach 60F. Meanwhile, the ECMWF is \nslower and shows temperatures reaching the mid 80s over the \nnortheast plains.\u00a0\n\nFor Tuesday through Friday, precipitation timing and chances \nremain uncertain due to model differences regarding the placement \nand track of the ejecting upper level low. Latest trends suggest a\nslower eastward progression of the trough. Thus, Tuesday is \nlooking drier. Temperatures will be cooler and closer to normal \nwith highs in the mid 60s to lower 70s over northeast Colorado. \nGiven the 12Z GFS and ECMWF, Tuesday night through Wednesday night\nis on track to be the best time period for precipitation as the \nupper level low begins to lift across the region.\u00a0There is still \ntremendous model uncertainty for Thursday and Friday. Models agree\ncooler air will remain over the western states with temperatures \nat or slightly below normal. As far as precipitation chances go, \nlow PoPs in the forecast seem on track given the large amount of \nuncertainty.",
